<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/elasticity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    When price rises, demand falls — but <strong>by how much?</strong> Elasticity is the answer:
    the percentage change in quantity per percentage change in price. <strong>Elastic</strong>
    goods (holidays, restaurant meals) shed buyers fast; <strong>inelastic</strong> goods
    (insulin, salt, fuel) barely flinch. The slope of the curve — literally — decides who bears
    a tax and whether a price rise earns money. Elasticity is the demand-and-supply lesson's
    sensitivity dial, and it upgrades every qualitative claim economics makes quantitative:
    "a price rise cuts demand" becomes "a 10% rise cuts demand by 4%, so revenue rises" —
    the difference between a proverb and a policy tool. This page covers the elasticity
    formula and why it uses percentages, the price elasticity of demand and its
    determinants, the revenue connection, price elasticity of supply, the other two
    elasticities (income and cross), tax incidence, and the role of time.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two demands, two personalities — raise price and watch which market walks away" widget="plotter" config={elascfg} />

  <p>
    The steep (inelastic) curve barely moves when price rises: sellers can pass taxes to buyers
    almost fully — which is why fuel taxes work and why insulin pricing scandalises economists.
    The flat (elastic) curve punishes any increase: luxury sellers eat their own taxes.
  </p>

  <h2>The formula: why percentages</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Price elasticity of demand (PED)</strong> is defined as
    <strong>PED = %Δ quantity demanded ÷ %Δ price</strong>. Using percentage changes rather
    than raw units is the design decision that makes the number portable: a 10% price
    rise means the same thing whether petrol is priced in pounds, dollars or litres —
    whereas "price rises 50p" depends entirely on the starting price and currency.
    The convention is to ignore the minus sign (quantity falls when price rises, so
    the raw number is negative) and compare magnitudes: <strong>|PED| greater than 1 is
    elastic</strong> (quantity responds more than proportionally), <strong>|PED| less than 1 is
    inelastic</strong> (less than proportionally), <strong>|PED| = 1 is unit elastic</strong>.
    <strong>A worked example:</strong> a café raises its flat white from £3.00 to £3.30 (+10%)
    and sales fall from 200 to 180 per day (−10%): PED = 10 ÷ 10 = 1, unit elastic —
    and, as the next section shows, revenue unchanged (600 either way). If sales had
    fallen to 150 (−25%), PED = 2.5, strongly elastic — the price rise backfired
    (revenue £600 → £495). If sales only fell to 190 (−5%), PED = 0.5, inelastic —
    the rise paid (£600 → £627). Note the elasticity is computed over a
    <em>range</em> (the "arc" between two points); along a straight-line demand curve
    the elasticity actually varies from highly elastic at high prices to highly
    inelastic at low ones, which is why textbook diagrams show elasticity regions
    along the curve rather than one number per curve.
  </p>

  <h2>Price elasticity of demand: the determinants</h2>
  <p>
    What decides whether buyers walk away? Five forces, each observable before any
    data is collected:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Substitutes:</strong> the master determinant — the easier the switch, the more elastic. One brand of cola is ferociously elastic (every rival is a substitute); cola as a category is much less so; drinks as a whole less again. <em>Market definition changes the answer.</em></li>
    <li><strong>Necessity versus luxury:</strong> insulin, electricity, staples are necessities bought whatever the price; cruises and restaurant meals can be postponed or skipped.</li>
    <li><strong>Share of income:</strong> a doubling of matchstick prices changes nobody's budget; a doubling of rent does. Big-ticket items are more elastic.</li>
    <li><strong>Habit and addiction:</strong> tobacco's demand is famously inelastic (empirical estimates cluster around −0.3 to −0.5), which is exactly why governments tax it — revenue rises, and consumption falls just enough to justify the public-health claim.</li>
    <li><strong>Time:</strong> the silent one — see below, it deserves its own section.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The empirical landscape is more nuanced than the categories suggest — petrol
    demand is roughly −0.25 in the short run but −0.6 or more over years, and even
    salt has substitutes if you include "use less" — but the pattern is robust:
    necessities without substitutes sit near zero, discretionary goods with
    alternatives stretch toward and beyond −1.
  </p>

  <h2>Elasticity and revenue: the seller's dial</h2>
  <p>
    Total revenue is price × quantity, and elasticity tells you which way it moves.
    <strong>Elastic demand:</strong> the quantity effect dominates — cut price and revenue
    rises, raise price and revenue falls (the luxury seller's trap).
    <strong>Inelastic demand:</strong> the price effect dominates — raise price and revenue
    rises (the necessity seller's power, and the state's tax base).
    <strong>Unit elastic:</strong> revenue is at its maximum — which is why revenue-
    maximising firms price near the unit-elastic point of their demand curve, and
    why the two-identical-numbers café above left revenue exactly unchanged.
    This one relationship powers an enormous amount of business and policy
    reasoning: premium pricing strategies (serve the inelastic segment),
    surge pricing (charge the inelastic moment), student and pensioner
    discounts (price-discriminate toward the elastic segments), and the
    design of cigarette taxes (revenue from inelasticity, harm reduction
    from the residual elasticity).
  </p>

  <h2>Price elasticity of supply</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>PES = %Δ quantity supplied ÷ %Δ price</strong> — how willingly producers
    respond to a price signal. The determinants are capacity-shaped:
    <strong>spare capacity</strong> (a factory running half-shifts can surge; a
    full one cannot), <strong>inventories</strong> (stocked goods supply elastically),
    <strong>production lags</strong> and <strong>factor mobility</strong> — and above all
    <strong>time</strong>. The classic case is agriculture: the momentary supply
    of this year's wheat is fixed (PES ≈ 0 — the crop is in the ground),
    the short run allows replanting decisions, and the long run allows
    land conversion. Housing is the modern textbook case: decades of
    restrictive planning make housing supply highly inelastic in
    high-demand cities, so demand growth converts almost entirely into
    price rather than quantity — the elasticity story behind "why is rent
    so high" in San Francisco or London. Fish and agricultural markets
    generally run low PES, manufactured goods with elastic supply adjust
    fast — and the policy consequences (buffer stocks, strategic
    reserves, agricultural subsidies) are all elasticity engineering.
  </p>

  <h2>Income and cross elasticity</h2>
  <p>
    Two further dials complete the family. <strong>Income elasticity of demand
    (YED)</strong> = %Δ quantity ÷ %Δ income. Positive YED marks a
    <strong>normal good</strong> (buy more when richer); within those, YED above 1
    marks a <strong>luxury</strong> (demand rises faster than income — holidays,
    restaurant meals); negative YED marks an <strong>inferior good</strong> (demand
    falls as income rises — bus travel relative to cars, own-brand
    staples relative to brands). YED explains recessions' strange
    beneficiaries: discount supermarkets and repair shops gain when
    incomes fall, which is why "inferior good" is a technical term, not
    an insult. <strong>Cross elasticity of demand (XED)</strong> = %Δ quantity of
    A ÷ %Δ price of B. Positive XED marks <strong>substitutes</strong> (Coke's sales
    rise when Pepsi's price rises); negative marks <strong>complements</strong>
    (car demand falls when fuel prices rise — and this is the linkage
    petrol-tax analysts exploit); near zero marks unrelated goods.
    XED is how competition authorities measure whether two firms are
    really in the same market: if a price rise at one sends customers
    to the other, the "market" is wider than the logos suggest.
  </p>

  <h2>Who bears a tax? Incidence and the slope</h2>
  <p>
    A tax's legal payer and its economic bearer are different people.
    Whoever <em>cannot walk away</em> bears it. <strong>Worked reasoning:</strong> put a
    £1 per unit tax on a good with inelastic demand and elastic supply:
    sellers try to pass it on, buyers barely cut back, so the market price
    rises by close to the full £1 — buyers pay. Reverse the elasticities
    (elastic demand, inelastic supply — say, perishable fish) and sellers
    eat most of the tax, because raising the posted price would empty the
    market. This incidence rule is one of economics' most reliable
    results: fuel taxes are paid by drivers (inelastic demand), payroll
    taxes land substantially on workers (inelastic labour supply),
    and taxes on elastic goods shrink their own base. The same arithmetic
    answers the subsidy question — a subsidy's benefit flows mostly to
    whichever side is less elastic — and explains why housing subsidies
    in supply-inelastic cities capitalise into prices rather than
    helping renters.
  </p>

  <h2>Time: the elasticity that grows</h2>
  <p>
    Elasticities are not constants; they are short-hand for behaviour under
    constraints, and constraints loosen with time. Fuel demand is inelastic
    <em>this month</em> (the commute is what it is, the car is bought) but more
    elastic <em>over years</em> (smaller cars, moves closer to work, public
    transport, working from home) — short-run estimates around −0.25
    stretch toward −0.6 and beyond in the long run. The policy lesson is
    sharp: a fuel tax raises revenue and barely dents consumption this
    year, then progressively reshapes the vehicle fleet — its "effect"
    depends entirely on the horizon. Congestion charges, sugar taxes and
    carbon prices all show the same profile: small immediate response,
    larger structural response, with the long-run number the only one
    that answers the policy question.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring elasticity in the wild</h2>
  <p>
    Estimated elasticities come from watching natural experiments, and the
    method is worth a glance because it explains why published numbers
    vary. The clean cases: a <strong>tax change</strong> that differs across a
    border or a date (cigarette taxes by US state) lets economists
    compare quantities before and after, treating the change as a
    market-level experiment; a <strong>surge-pricing episode</strong> or an
    <strong>airline yield system</strong> generates price variation within days
    on identical seats. The estimates then feed back into policy
    arithmetic: the "elasticity of demand for cigarettes" (about
    −0.4 overall, but −0.7 or more for teenagers, whose smoking is
    more discretionary) is the number that decides how much a tobacco
    tax both earns and deters — the adolescent figure being the one
    public-health campaigns buy. The same machinery priced the
    "necessity" boundary empirically: electricity demand is inelastic
    in the short run (≈ −0.1) and much more elastic across decades
    as appliances and insulation turn over — time-series versus
    cross-section estimates disagree precisely because the
    horizon differs, which is the time lesson wearing
    measurement clothes.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Where elasticity came from.</strong> Alfred Marshall formalised it in
    <em>Principles of Economics</em> (1890), giving economics its first portable
    sensitivity measure; the modern toolkit (point elasticity using
    calculus, arc elasticity between observations, econometric estimation
    from natural experiments) extends the same idea. Estimates have
    improved with data but the rankings Marshall drew — necessities
    inelastic, luxuries elastic — have not moved.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The agricultural paradox, formalised.</strong> With inelastic demand
    and inelastic short-run supply, small supply shifts produce large
    price swings — cobweb models show how one-year-lag supply responses
    can generate oscillating booms and busts in hog and coffee markets,
    and why commodity stabilisation schemes exist. Coffee blight,
    oil shocks and the paradox of plenty are one phenomenon wearing
    different hats.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Elasticity in digital markets defies the old
    intuitions — a software copy has near-zero marginal cost, so
    pricing runs on value and discrimination rather than cost, and
    "supply" is nearly perfectly elastic. Attention markets (the price
    of your time on a feed) have elasticities nobody has measured well.
    And the biggest empirical programme running is carbon: the social
    cost of carbon policy depends on the long-run elasticity of energy
    demand, and estimates still vary enough to change policy conclusions.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Marshall, Principles of Economics (1890); OpenStax, Principles of Economics (Elasticity); empirical estimates from tobacco and fuel-demand literatures.</p>

